 is Likely R (10 of 10 raters at Lean R or better for Republicans)", "July polls showed Turner up 7 points; no recent polling", "Polymarket prices Knickerbocker around 14%"], "rationale": "Turner's incumbency, 2.5-to-1 fundraising edge, and the district's Republican lean align with a Likely R consensus among all ten expert raters. July Democratic-sponsored polls showed a 7-point Turner lead, and markets price his win odds near 83%, supporting a margin around R+8.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a major external-spending push against Turner could narrow the race toward Lean R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 81 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Turner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Mike Turner seeks another term in this Dayton-area district that leans Republican by about four points. He has out-raised Democrat Kristina Knickerbocker roughly $895,000 to $363,000 and holds a large cash advantage heading into the final weeks. Every major race rater places the seat at Likely R or safer for Republicans, and prediction markets give Turner roughly an 83% chance of winning.\n\nThe only public polling, conducted in July by Democratic sponsors, showed Turner ahead by about seven points among likely voters, though it is now several months old. Knickerbocker's campaign has highlighted Turner's war powers vote, but there is little evidence of a competitive race. A Libertarian candidate appears on the ballot with minimal funding. The forecast expects Turner to win by a solid but not overwhelming margin, with moderate uncertainty given the age of available polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1787, "margin": -5.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1387, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1203, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.155, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1216, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.119, "margin": -7.0}, {"date"